# The desktop app holds no AI credentials. It calls the web app's /api/ai
# routes, which own the OpenAI key and check the user's licence. Anything
# bundled into a renderer can be read straight out of the app folder, so there
# is deliberately nothing to put here.

# Copy this to `.env` OR to `.env.local` — electron/main.cjs loads both, and
# `.env.local` wins where they disagree. Until 2026-09-01 only `.env` was read,
# so a `.env.local` written out of Next.js habit was ignored and the app talked
# to production while looking like it was talking to localhost.

# Backend that validates licences and serves the AI routes.
# Defaults to the deployed app; point it at a local web build during development.
# Use 127.0.0.1, not localhost: Electron's main process runs Node, which
# resolves localhost to ::1 and will not fall back to IPv4.
#
# SPLIT 2026-09-01: this is the APP origin, not the marketing site. The two are
# separate deployments now — smarthire.ai serves marketing and nothing the
# desktop app can use. Every endpoint below lives on the app origin:
#   /api/license/{validate,stream}  /api/session/{start,heartbeat,stop}
#   /api/profiles  /api/ai/{models,chat,transcribe,realtime}
# plus the pages openExternal is allowed to open: /signup, /dashboard/*.
WEB_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000
